{"request": {"input_type": "document", "model": "voyage-4", "text": "The head of sales explains how the team writes prospect emails: short and friendly for small companies, more formal for mid-sized firms, never putting discounts in writing, and quoting catalog list prices exactly (Starter 490 euros, Pro 1490).\n\nInterviewer: Thanks for making the time. Let's start with how your team writes to prospects.\n\nHead of sales: Sure. Most of our customers are small companies, maybe twenty people, and the person reading our email is often the founder. So with small companies we keep emails short and friendly. We use first names, and we stop after five sentences. If it needs more than that, it should be a call.\n\nInterviewer: And for larger accounts?\n\nHead of sales: Mid-sized firms get a more formal email, but honestly the bigger rule is about money. We never put a discount in writing in an email to a customer. Discounts are discussed on the phone, with the account owner, and then they show up in the order form. An email gets forwarded to finance and suddenly the discount is the new price.\n\nInterviewer: What about prices in general?\n\nHead of sales: When we mention a price, we always quote the list price from the catalog, exactly as it is. No rounding, no \"around five hundred\". Starter is 490 euros, Pro is 1490, and that is what the email says. If someone wants a bundle, the account manager builds a quote, the email just points to it."}, "vector": [0.02029307559132576, -0.013439138419926167, -0.03904222324490547, -0.035633571445941925, 0.007239893544465303, -0.00699253473430872, -0.03708886727690697, -0.01710883155465126, 0.008826762437820435, 0.03122585453093052, -0.009165691211819649, 0.06770909577608109, 0.0012504004407674074, 0.018981562927365303, -0.02989540435373783, 0.030787866562604904, -0.02770928665995598, -0.012007225304841995, 0.03847675770521164, 0.04296095669269562, 0.0203701201826334, 0.057435061782598495, -0.0215716902166605, -0.025055747479200363, 